  "question": {
    "text": "A rod&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AB</mi></math> of length&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> and mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> is free to move on a frictionless horizontal surface. It is moving with a velocity&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math>, as shown in figure. End&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> of rod&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AB</mi></math> strikes the end of the wall. Assuming elastic impact, the angular velocity of the rod&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AB</mi></math>, just after impact, is<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0094_ecf54e424c69.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 74px;\" />"
  },
